\begin_layout Title
Special Topics: Field Experiments/Neuroeconomics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
November 14, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Increasingly, economists have turned to experiments in order to understand
 human behavior.
 The readings this week specifically cover two special topics associated
 with experimental economics: field experiments and neuroeconomics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Field experiments are becoming a popular approach to explore economic phenomena.
 Field experiments use randomization, but do so in naturally-ocurring settings.
 These type of experiments incorporate a mixture of control and realism,
 so they provide a bridge between laboratory and naturally-occurring data.
 Levitt and List (2009) present an overview of modern field experiments
 and their usage in economics.
 In their study, they discuss some of the first field-experiments, as well
 as some large-scale social experiments.
 Then, they provide a description of the current field experiments seen
 today.
 Finally, the authors offer a conjecture of where the frontier of field
 experiments will soon be headed.
 Charness and Gneezy (2009) is a prime example of what a proper field experiment
 entails.
 The authors wish to see if incentives are an effective strategy in encouraging
 the development of good habits.
 They do this by analyzing the post-intervention effects of paying people
 to attend a gym a number of times during one month.
 In two studies, they find an increase in attendance after intervention
 relative to the respective control groups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Neuroeconomics research requires curiosity in not only economics, but also
 neurobiology.
 It combines research methods from neurobiology, experimental and behavioral
 economics, and cognititive and social psychology.
 Neuroeconomics seeks to explain how economic behavior can shape our understandi
ng of the brain.
 With new neuroscientific discoveries, new economics models can be formed,
 or existing theories can be improved.
 Bechara et al.
 (1997) and Plassmann et al.
 (2008) are two examples of neuroeconomic research.
 Bechara et al.
 (1997) sought out to determine whether overt reasoning is preceded by a
 nonconscious response.
 They found that normal individuals began to choose advantageously before
 they realized which strategy worked best.
 Plassman et al.
 (2008) studied the neural mechanisms through which marketing affects decisions
 made by individuals.
 They showed that marketing actions, especially changes in price, can affect
 neural representations of experienced pleasantness
